ios: canvases get only the pixels that changed

Every frame the client converted the whole world (812x375 at the HUD's size) and the minimap to RGBA and sent them
with putImageData, about 100 pushes and 65 MB a second, though with a still camera 0.3-0.5 % of the world's pixels
change and the minimap is identical in about two frames of three. In WebKit each push costs the GPU process a
conversion and an IOSurface. A PixMap that owns its canvas (the mobile layout's surfaces, patch 11) now keeps the frame
it last sent (`shown`): draw() compares row by row, converts only the changed pixels and sends their bounding rect
(putImageData's dirty rect), or nothing when no pixel changed. The title screen's PixMaps do the same once the client
has loaded (`changesOnly`): each owns its part of the classic canvas, the login box is drawn every frame and mostly
unchanged, the flames change in places.

The canvas still ends each draw with exactly the frame's pixels. The whole frame is sent on a PixMap's first draw,
after a retarget or a move, every 100th draw, and after PixMap.invalidate(): on visibilitychange and pageshow (WebKit
may drop a hidden page's canvas buffers), when a canvas's context is restored (blank again), at a full redraw
(redrawFrame), and when the canvas is drawn directly (the plain loading bar, the error screen, a resize).

On Android (lcHost.platform 'android': Chromium's WebView) a PixMap sends its whole frame when any of its pixels
changed, as upstream sends every frame, and nothing when none did: the rect made in-game frames stall there. On the
Android emulator (3 runs each), frames of 100 ms or more a minute, rect / whole frames when changed / upstream: Lumbridge
standing 159 / 18 / 50, walking 186 / 30 / 64; Varrock West with 500 bots standing 152 / 30 / 42, walking 170 / 49 /
90. iOS, and a page without an app (the desktop tests), sends the rect.

